// One function's failure does not silence the next function's flow analysis.
//
// fnDclTypeCheck used to run blockFlow only while the global error count was
// zero, so a single error anywhere earlier in the compile -- of any stage, in
// any function -- turned off immutability checking, ErrorMove and the lifetime
// checks for everything after it. A whole class of rule was enforced only in
// files that were otherwise clean, which is how 'imm' came to look unenforced
// when it had been checked since 2021.
//
// The gate is now per function: it compares the error count against the one on
// entry, so a function whose own signature and body type checked is analyzed
// whatever failed elsewhere. This file is the assertion. The first function
// fails its type check and gets no flow analysis; the second and third type
// check cleanly and report their flow diagnostics anyway.
//
// Two other gates are deliberately still global and are not what this asserts:
// name resolution returns before type checking begins if it reported anything
// (conec.c), and a module type checks no body if any signature failed
// (module.c). Both are why the first function's error must be in its body.

fn bodyFailsTypeCheck() i32 {
  true                       //~ ErrorInvType:3 "Return expression type does not match return type on function"
                             //~^^ ErrorInvType:25 "This is the declared function's return type" follow-on
}

// Reported only because the gate is per function now
fn immStillChecked() i32 {
  imm n = 3
  n = 5                      //~ ErrorNoMut:3 "You do not have permission to modify lval"
  n
}

// ... and a second offending function is reported too, where before only the
// first function to reach flow analysis ever was
fn immCheckedAgain() i32 {
  imm m = 7
  m = 9                      //~ ErrorNoMut:3 "You do not have permission to modify lval"
  m
}
